# Nanotron — environment template.
#
# Copy to .env.local (git-ignored) and fill in. Never commit real credentials:
# .env.local and every other .env file are ignored, and this template is the
# only one tracked.
#
# CLASSIFICATION — which of these the browser can see
# ---------------------------------------------------
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_*        inlined into the client bundle at build time. Public by
#                        design. Nothing secret may ever carry this prefix.
#   everything else      server-only. Read in `server-only` modules, sent as
#                        request headers where needed, never logged.
#   DEV_*                local development only. Read by a script, or by the
#                        local test sign-in, which only exists in `next dev`
#                        on localhost. Never set these on a server.
#
# Leave DATABASE_URL unset to run on the seed data in src/data: catalogue and
# platform reads fall back, while user-scoped reads and every write refuse
# rather than serving one demo person's figures to whoever is asking.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connections
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two, because the application and the migration scripts want different things
# from a connection. See CLAUDE.md §16.1.

# Runtime. Pooled, shared by every render.
#   Supabase : the SESSION pooler, port 5432 —
#              postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require
#   local    : postgresql://nanotron:password@localhost:5432/nanotron
#
# On Supabase, do NOT use db.<ref>.supabase.co — it resolves over IPv6 only and
# Vercel's serverless runtime has no IPv6 egress.
#
# And do NOT use the transaction pooler on :6543, even though it is the usual
# serverless advice. postgres.js pipelines queries and Supavisor cannot
# demultiplex that, so concurrent queries hang; the only driver setting that
# stops the pipelining (max_pipeline: 0) also stops postgres.js reserving a
# connection for sql.begin, which breaks every transaction — and every write
# here is a transaction. Measured, with numbers, in CLAUDE.md §16.1.
DATABASE_URL=

# Migrations, seeding and Drizzle Studio. A separate, single, short-lived
# connection. A migration takes an advisory lock and issues DDL, which wants a
# session to itself rather than a share of the render pool. Optional — falls
# back to DATABASE_URL.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tuning (all optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Force TLS on or off. Left unset, TLS follows the URL's sslmode.
# DATABASE_SSL=

# Maximum pooled connections PER SERVER INSTANCE. Default 5.
#
# The Supabase session pooler refuses a sixteenth client for the whole project
# ("max clients are limited to pool_size: 15"), and that budget is shared with
# the db:* scripts, the TRON scanner and the test suite. Five per instance
# leaves room for two instances plus the scripts. Three was tried and is too
# tight: eighteen concurrent authenticated requests took 14.3s at three against
# 8.4–9.3s at five. Raise it above five only for a deployment that really is a
# single long-running server, or after raising the project's pool size in the
# Supabase dashboard.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=

# Connections opened in the background when the pool is created. Default 3.
# Moves with DATABASE_POOL_MAX — raising one without the other is worse than
# raising neither. Set DATABASE_WARMUP=false to skip warming entirely.
# DATABASE_WARM_CONNECTIONS=

# How long an unused pooled connection is kept, in seconds. Default 120.
#
# NOT ZERO. On the session pooler a held connection reserves a Postgres backend,
# and the project's ceiling is fifteen clients in total. `0` means a connection
# is never given back, so every process that ever queries — a local `next
# start`, each Vercel instance, a test run — permanently claims up to
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX of that budget until it exits. That is `EMAXCONNSESSION`,
# and it happens at zero traffic. See CLAUDE.md §16.1a.
#
# It was 30, and 30 was too short. Measured 2026-09-18 against a production
# build: a 35s pause and then one navigation cost 4.5–8.3s, against 0.8–2.1s
# at 120 — because the interval that governs is how long somebody spends
# *reading* a screen, not how fast they click. 120 still hands connections
# back, two minutes after the last query, so the budget is shared over time.
#
# Lower it toward 30 if this deployment routinely runs three or more
# concurrent instances: an idle instance then holds its slots for two minutes,
# which is the cost of the change.
#
# ON A DEVELOPMENT MACHINE, SET THIS TO 30. A laptop routinely runs more
# "instances" than a deployment does: `next dev`, a `next start` you left up to
# measure against, `npm test`, `npm run db:*` and `npm run tron:scan` are five
# separate Node processes, and each one claims up to DATABASE_POOL_MAX of the
# project's fifteen session-mode slots and keeps them for this many seconds
# after its last query. At 120 three of them are enough to reach the ceiling
# and refuse the fourth with (EMAXCONNSESSION); at 30 they drain between uses.
# DATABASE_IDLE_TIMEOUT=

# How long a read may take before it is treated as an outage. Default 15000ms.
# Lower it on a serverless platform, where a hung invocation is billed.
# DATABASE_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS=

# Open connections over IPv4 only. Default false. Set this if `getaddrinfo`
# on your machine stalls for seconds on AAAA lookups — the symptom is that
# every new connection takes ~5s and pages time out under parallel queries.
#
# It cannot rescue a network that has no IPv4 at all. Supabase's pooler
# publishes only A records, so on an IPv6-only link (a mobile connection whose
# 464XLAT/CLAT translation has dropped, say) every connection fails with
# ENETUNREACH no matter what this is set to — observed on this project, with
# `ip -4 route` empty and the only IPv4 address on the machine being loopback.
# That is a local network fault, not an application one; `ip -4 -br addr` and
# `ip route get 8.8.8.8` tell you which you are looking at in one second.
# DATABASE_FORCE_IPV4=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE-TIME SETUP, AFTER `npm run db:migrate`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#     npm run db:secure
#
# Enables row level security on every application table and provisions the
# private `kyc-documents` bucket with its access policies. Both are Supabase
# configuration a Drizzle migration cannot express, and both use the connection
# above — no service-role key.
#
# WITHOUT IT, EVERY TABLE IS READABLE BY ANYONE HOLDING THE ANON KEY. Supabase
# publishes `public` over PostgREST and grants `anon` SELECT; Drizzle creates
# tables with RLS off. Run it after every migration that adds a table. See
# CLAUDE.md §16.1b.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase Auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From the Supabase dashboard: Project Settings → API.
#
# These two are the ONLY Supabase values the browser may see. The anon key is
# public by design — it identifies the project, not a user.
#
# The service-role key is deliberately absent. It bypasses row-level security,
# so a copy in the browser is a copy of the database. Nothing here needs it:
# privileged work runs server-side over DATABASE_URL, a separate credential.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The publishable (formerly "anon") key. Both spellings are read, newest first,
# so a project created under either naming works without an edit. They are the
# same credential; set one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# WHAT MUST BE CONFIGURED IN THE SUPABASE DASHBOARD, NOT HERE
# -----------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication → Providers → Email      enable Email; enable Email OTP if you
#                                         want the one-time-code sign-in option
# Authentication → Emails → SMTP          production host, port, user, password,
#                                         sender. The built-in sender is rate
#                                         limited to a handful of messages an
#                                         hour and is for development only.
# Authentication → Emails → Templates     the Magic Link template must contain
#                                         {{ .Token }} for the code option
# Authentication → URL Configuration      Site URL, and add
#                                         <origin>/auth/callback to the redirect
#                                         allow-list — every emailed link lands
#                                         there, and Supabase honours only
#                                         allow-listed origins
#
# SMTP credentials stay in Supabase so this application never holds them and
# cannot leak them. SMTP is *email*; mobile one-time codes would need an SMS
# provider, which is a separate integration. See CLAUDE.md §19.3.
#
# The service-role key is deliberately absent and must not be added. It bypasses
# row-level security, so a copy of it anywhere in this project is a copy of the
# database. Its absence is why the CRM cannot revoke a Supabase session
# server-side, and the UI says so rather than pretending otherwise.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development sign-in credentials — LOCAL ONLY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by `npm run db:dev-accounts`, never at runtime. That script creates the
# Supabase user through the ordinary public sign-up endpoint (not the admin API,
# which would need the service-role key) and links it to the seeded master
# admin. It refuses under NODE_ENV=production and never prints a password.
# (Customers no longer have an email login, so there is no DEV_TEST_EMAIL.)
#
# Put real values in .env.local, which is git-ignored. Never here.
#
# If Supabase's email rate limit blocks account creation, or somebody has
# already registered through the sign-up form, use the no-email path instead:
#
#     npm run db:link-admin -- someone@example.com
#
# which copies the auth principal that account already carries onto the master
# admin row. No credential is created and no email is sent.
DEV_ADMIN_EMAIL=
DEV_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# LOCAL TEST SIGN-IN — `next dev` on localhost only (src/server/auth/dev-test-auth.ts).
# Replaces the SMS / emailed code with a fixed one, for one test customer and
# the dev operator above. It is compiled out of production builds, and it also
# needs DEV_TEST_AUTH=true and a localhost Host header. Choose a test number
# that is not a real person's; it only ever exists in your dev database.
# DEV_TEST_AUTH=true
# DEV_TEST_CUSTOMER_PHONE=
# DEV_TEST_CUSTOMER_OTP=        (six digits)
# DEV_TEST_ADMIN_OTP=           (six digits; signs in as DEV_ADMIN_EMAIL)
# DEV_TEST_OTP_TTL_SECONDS=300  (how long a test code stays valid)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRON
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-side only. None of these is prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, so none of them
# reaches the browser bundle — the API key in particular must not.
#
# Leave TRON_USDT_CONTRACT and TRON_PLATFORM_DEPOSIT_ADDRESS unset to run
# without the chain integration; the rest of the application is unaffected.

# "mainnet", "shasta" or "nile".
#
# UNSET MEANS "shasta", NOT MAINNET. Reaching real money requires naming the
# network, and on mainnet confirmation cannot be disabled (see
# TRON_CONFIRMATIONS below) — crediting before a block is solidified credits
# money a re-org can reverse.
TRON_NETWORK=mainnet

# Any of these spellings is accepted for the grid URL; TRONGRID_API_URL takes
# precedence, then TRON_GRID_API_URL, then TRON_GRID_URL. Unset falls back to
# the public endpoint for the configured network.
#   mainnet  https://api.trongrid.io
#   shasta   https://api.shasta.trongrid.io
#   nile     https://nile.trongrid.io
TRONGRID_API_URL=https://api.trongrid.io
# TRON_GRID_API_URL=
# TRON_GRID_URL=

# Optional in principle; in practice get one for mainnet — the unauthenticated
# rate limit will not sustain the deposit screen's polling. Sent as a request
# header, never as a query parameter, and never logged.
TRONGRID_API_KEY=
# TRON_GRID_API_KEY=

# The TRC-20 contract to accept. Anyone can deploy a token called USDT and
# send it to the deposit address; only this contract counts as a deposit.
#
# TRON mainnet USDT (Tether) is TR7NHqjeKQxGTCi8q8ZY4pL8otSzgjLj6t. Check it
# against a block explorer before pasting it anywhere: the wrong contract here
# means either detecting nothing, or crediting a worthless token as if it were
# money.
TRON_USDT_CONTRACT=

# The deployment's DEFAULT receiving address — the one address customers send
# USDT (TRC-20) to until an operator saves a different one in the CRM
# (Deposits → Deposit configuration). A saved address takes precedence; this
# stays watched by the scanner either way. Optional once one is saved.
# Customers never see this variable: the deposit screen reads the active
# address server-side. See CLAUDE.md §18.4 and §18.8.
TRON_DEPOSIT_ADDRESS=
# TRON_PLATFORM_DEPOSIT_ADDRESS=

# How long a deposit request reserves its exact amount, in minutes (15–1440).
# A transfer is matched to a request only inside this window. Default 60.
# DEPOSIT_REQUEST_TTL_MINUTES=60

# Wait for the transaction's block to solidify (become irreversible) before
# recording it as creditable.
#
# Any value above zero means "wait for the block to solidify". TRON has no
# confirmation count in the Bitcoin sense — see CLAUDE.md §18.3.
#
# ON MAINNET THIS CANNOT BE TURNED OFF. Zero (or TRON_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED
# =false) is refused by getTronConfig when TRON_NETWORK=mainnet, because it
# would credit real money a re-org can still take back. It remains available on
# a testnet, where waiting on solidification only slows a test down.
TRON_CONFIRMATIONS=1
# TRON_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The scheduled deposit scan
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GET /api/cron/scan-deposits runs one scanner pass. The scheduler sends this
# value as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>`. On EC2 that is the crontab in
# deploy/ec2/nanotron.cron; on Vercel, vercel.json.
#
# UNSET MEANS THE ROUTE REFUSES EVERYBODY, including the scheduler. That is
# deliberate: the endpoint spends TronGrid quota and database connections, and
# an open one is a free way to exhaust both. Deposits are then only detected by
# somebody running `npm run tron:scan` by hand.
#
# Any long random string (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`).
CRON_SECRET=

# Scanner polling, for `npm run tron:scan -- --watch`. Optional.
# TRON_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=30000
# How far back a poll looks when there is no cursor yet. Code default 24h.
#
# SET TO 72h WHILE THE SCHEDULE IS DAILY. The code default is exactly one day,
# which is also the cron cadence on Vercel's Hobby plan (see vercel.json and
# CLAUDE.md §18.5) — and Hobby honours a scheduled time only to within the
# hour, so two consecutive passes can be nearly 26 hours apart. An address
# whose cursor has never seen a transfer falls back to this window on every
# pass, so at 24h one late or failed pass can put a transfer permanently out of
# reach. 72h costs one extra TronGrid page and removes the edge entirely.
#
# Restore 86400000 when an external scheduler runs the scan more than daily.
TRON_LOOKBACK_MS=259200000
# TRON_PAGE_SIZE=50

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity verification
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A KYC submission always requires an identity document AND a live photo
# (server-enforced, `@/server/services/kyc-policy`). Phone-signed-in customers
# can only upload to S3, so production needs the KYC_STORAGE_DRIVER=s3 block
# below; without it the verification screen says it is unavailable.
# (KYC_REQUIRE_DOCUMENTS / NEXT_PUBLIC_KYC_REQUIRE_DOCUMENTS no longer exist.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firebase — customer phone OTP sign-in (CLAUDE.md §19)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PUBLIC: the web app config from Firebase console → Project settings → Your
# apps → Web app. Inlined into the browser bundle by design; it identifies the
# project and authorises nothing on its own. Protection is console
# configuration: authorised domains, SMS region policy (India only), App Check.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
# Optional for Auth; passed through when present.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=

# No Firebase service-account key is used anywhere. The server verifies the
# ID token Firebase issues after an OTP with the PUBLIC project id alone, then
# issues its own signed session. Optional: pin the server to a project id
# explicitly (defaults to NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID).
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=

# SERVER-ONLY SECRET: signs customer session cookies. At least 32 characters;
# generate with `openssl rand -base64 48`. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_, never committed.
# Changing it signs every customer out (the emergency lever).
CUSTOMER_SESSION_SECRET=

# Customer session length in days, 1–14. Default 14.
# CUSTOMER_SESSION_DAYS=14
#
# With the web config, the project id or the secret missing, /login says phone
# sign-in is unavailable. Nothing fails silently.

# LEGACY customer email sign-in (/login/email, /signup, /forgot-password). Off
# unless "true". Turn it on only for a migration window, so a customer whose
# account predates phone sign-in can sign in once and link a verified number
# (/link-phone). Operators are not affected by this.
# LEGACY_EMAIL_SIGN_IN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Customer support
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No variable. The Telegram support destination is set by an operator in
# Admin → Settings → Customer support and stored in platform_settings; the
# customer app reads it from there (src/lib/support.ts validates it).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KYC document storage — private AWS S3 (CLAUDE.md §16.1c)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OFF until all three are set. There is no default bucket: nothing uploads
# anywhere unless you name one here.
# KYC_STORAGE_DRIVER=s3
# KYC_S3_BUCKET=
# KYC_S3_REGION=ap-south-1
# Optional: key prefix (default "kyc") and a KMS key for SSE-KMS (default SSE-S3).
# KYC_S3_PREFIX=kyc
# KYC_S3_KMS_KEY_ID=
#
# CREDENTIALS: prefer the EC2 instance role — the AWS SDK finds it via IMDSv2
# and no key exists to leak. Only if that is impossible, set the standard
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY here (server-only; never
# NEXT_PUBLIC_). KYC_STORAGE_DRIVER=disabled turns uploads off explicitly.
